WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.540
AI governance has to follow systems through their lifecycle.

2
00:00:03.640 --> 00:00:20.920
A useful lifecycle model moves from concept and feasibility to requirements, acquisition or development, data preparation, training or configuration, verification and validation, deployment approval, operation, monitoring, change, and retirement.

3
00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.900
Deployment is not the finish line.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.640
It is the point where operational evidence begins.

5
00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:34.660
A model that was acceptable at approval can become unsuitable after data, users, suppliers, or business processes change.

6
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.900
Not every organization trains its own model.

7
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:47.120
One business may develop a model, another may fine-tune one, another may configure an external service, and another may use an AI feature embedded in ordinary software.

8
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:49.300
The governance responsibility remains.

9
00:00:49.400 --> 00:01:03.400
The organization still decides intended purpose, acceptable use, ownership, data sharing, monitoring, escalation, supplier controls, and whether outputs can influence business decisions.

10
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.060
Procurement is therefore part of lifecycle governance, not a separate shortcut around it.

11
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.500
Lifecycle governance starts with intended purpose and requirements.

12
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:25.480
Requirements should address functional expectations, constraints, data needs, security, privacy, human oversight, acceptance criteria, misuse resistance, monitoring, and change control.

13
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.900
Verification and validation should not collapse into one accuracy number.

14
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:43.840
Depending on context, evaluation may consider reliability, robustness, uneven performance, explainability, human factors, accessibility, fail-safe behavior, privacy, security, and operational constraints.

15
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:47.860
Acceptance criteria should be approved before results are interpreted.

16
00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:50.940
Data governance deserves focused attention.

17
00:01:51.040 --> 00:02:10.560
The organization should understand provenance, authorized access, collection context, label quality, relevance, missing data, representativeness, transformation history, versioning, lineage, access controls, retention, deletion, supplier-provided data, synthetic data, and drift.

18
00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:20.420
Lineage is especially important when a system changes because it helps the team understand which data, model, configuration, and decision process produced a given outcome.

19
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.560
Without lineage, monitoring problems can become investigations without a trail.

20
00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.400
Change management is where many AI governance programs become real.

21
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:46.340
Model updates, prompt or configuration changes, data changes, provider updates, new use cases, new affected populations, integration changes, monitoring-threshold changes, and new obligations may trigger reassessment.

22
00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:58.040
Retirement also requires control: disable use, preserve records, remove access, address retained data, replace dependent processes, and monitor residual obligations.

23
00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.700
AI governance is lifecycle evidence, not launch approval alone.

24
00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:14.960
The practical discipline is to define gates before launch, preserve evidence during operation, reassess material change, and close systems deliberately when they no longer fit their approved purpose.

25
00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:21.700
That discipline applies whether the organization builds, buys, configures, or merely enables the AI capability.

26
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:27.960
It also keeps supplier changes and internal configuration changes from bypassing governance.
